1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome back to the VentureX tutorial series.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,500
In this video, we're diving into the CRM and
Sales Pipeline modules —

3
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:11,000
the heart of your customer
relationship management.

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:15,000
We'll cover companies, contacts,
activities, and how to manage

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:18,500
your entire sales funnel from
lead to close.

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,500
Let's start with the CRM module.
VentureX organizes your customer data

7
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:26,000
into three core components:
Companies — the organizations you do

8
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:29,500
business with, Contacts — the people
within those companies,

9
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,000
and Activities — every interaction
you've logged.

10
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:35,000
Everything connects, giving you
a 360-degree view of every relationship.

11
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:39,000
Let's add our first company.
I'll click on Companies in the sidebar,

12
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:42,500
and since this is a fresh install,
we see the empty state.

13
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:45,500
Let's click the Add Company button.

14
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:49,500
The form asks for the essentials.
Company name — I'll enter Acme Corporation.

15
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,000
Industry — let's select Technology.
Website — acme-corp.com.

16
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:57,000
Phone, email, address — all optional
but recommended.

17
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:01,000
Notice the custom fields section at
the bottom — you can add your own data

18
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:04,500
points like Company Size or
Annual Revenue.

19
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:07,500
Let me fill in a few of these.

20
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:11,500
Before saving, notice the Tags field —
these are great for segmenting companies

21
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,000
later. I'll add 'Enterprise'
and 'Active Client'.

22
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:16,500
Now let's save.

23
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:20,000
Now let's add a contact — a person
at Acme Corporation.

24
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:23,000
I'll navigate to Contacts
and click Add Contact.

25
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:27,000
First name — Sarah. Last name — Mitchell.
The Company field is a searchable dropdown

26
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:30,500
that pulls from our Companies module.
Watch this —

27
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:34,000
I'll start typing Acme and
it auto-completes.

28
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:38,000
This creates a relational link between
the contact and the company.

29
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:42,000
Email, phone, title — I'll set her
as VP of Sales.

30
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:46,500
We can also assign an Account Owner —
the VentureX user responsible

31
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,000
for this relationship.

32
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:52,500
Same custom fields and tags system
as Companies.

33
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,500
Let me add the tag 'Decision Maker'
and save this contact.

34
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:59,500
Activities are how you track every
touchpoint. Calls, emails, meetings,

35
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:03,000
notes — everything lives here and
can be tied to a specific contact

36
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:05,500
or company.
Let's log an activity for Sarah.

37
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:09,500
Activity type — I'll choose Call.
Subject: Introductory Call.

38
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:13,000
Date and time: let's set it to today.
Duration: 30 minutes.

39
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:17,500
I'll add a note: 'Discussed Acme's
current ERP pain points.

40
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:21,000
They're using spreadsheets and
losing data.

41
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:25,000
Interested in demo of
inventory module.'

42
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:28,000
The Activity Status — Completed.

43
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,500
We can also link this to a Deal,
but we haven't created one yet.

44
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:34,500
We'll come back to that.

45
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:38,500
When I save this, notice how it
appears in Sarah's activity timeline.

46
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:42,000
Every future activity, email,
and deal will stack here chronologically.

47
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:45,000
Let's add one more — a meeting.

48
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:49,000
From the main Activities page, you can
see all activities across all contacts

49
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:52,000
and companies. You can filter by type,
date range, status,

50
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:55,500
or assigned user. This is your
activity feed —

51
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:58,500
your single source of truth
for every interaction.

52
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:02,500
Now let's move to the Sales Pipeline —
this is where the magic happens.

53
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:06,500
VentureX uses a Kanban-style board
where each column represents

54
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:10,000
a pipeline stage. Out of the box,
you get: Prospecting, Qualification,

55
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:14,000
Proposal, Negotiation, Closed Won,
and Closed Lost.

56
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:17,000
You can customize these stages
in Settings.

57
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:21,000
Let's create a deal for
Acme Corporation.

58
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:24,500
I'll click New Deal. Deal name:
'Acme — Enterprise ERP Suite'.

59
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:28,000
Company: Acme Corporation —
the auto-complete is here too.

60
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:31,500
Contact: Sarah Mitchell.
Stage: Prospecting.

61
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:35,000
Deal value: I'll set this at $45,000.
Expected close date: end of next month.

62
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:39,000
Probability — this auto-fills based on
the stage. For Prospecting, it defaults

63
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:42,000
to 10%. I can override it
manually if I have a gut feeling

64
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:44,500
this is more likely.

65
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:48,000
Optional fields include Assigned To,
Priority, and a Description.

66
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:51,500
I'll set Priority to High and add
a brief description. Let's save this deal.

67
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:55,500
Here's our deal in the Prospecting stage.
Now watch this —

68
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:59,000
I'm going to drag it to Qualification.
This is the beauty of Kanban:

69
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:02,500
visual, intuitive, and instant.

70
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:06,500
When I click into the deal, you can see
all the details, activity history,

71
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:10,000
and the linked contact and company.
The Activity Log shows our introductory call.

72
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:13,500
Let me log a follow-up activity
directly from here, then move this

73
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:16,000
deal forward.

74
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:20,000
Great demo happened, so I'm moving this
to Proposal stage.

75
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:23,500
Probability jumps to 40%.
Let's open it and log the proposal sent.

76
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:27,500
They're negotiating on pricing.
Moving to Negotiation —

77
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:30,000
probability now at 60%.

78
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:34,000
And... they signed! Moving to
Closed Won. The probability is 100%,

79
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:37,500
the deal value is locked in,
and this now shows in your revenue reports.

80
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:41,500
Let's look at the numbers.
At the top of the pipeline, you can see

81
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:45,000
summary metrics — total pipeline value,
weighted pipeline value based on

82
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:48,500
probability, number of deals per stage,
and your win rate.

83
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:52,000
Let's create a second deal so these
numbers are more interesting.

84
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:56,000
Second deal: 'TechStart — Starter CRM
Package', Value: $8,000,

85
00:04:56,100 --> 00:04:59,000
Stage: Qualification, Probability: 30%.

86
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:03,000
Now the pipeline shows $53,000 in total
value, with a weighted forecast

87
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:06,500
of about $44,100. This weighted number
is what you'd use for revenue forecasting —

88
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:10,000
it accounts for the probability of
each deal closing.

89
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:14,000
VentureX includes built-in sales reports.
You can view deals by stage,

90
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:17,500
by rep, by time period, and by value.
The pipeline visualization shows

91
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:21,000
conversion rates between stages —
how many prospects become qualified,

92
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:24,500
how many proposals become negotiations,
and ultimately, your close rate.

93
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:28,000
These reports update in real-time
as you move deals through the pipeline.

94
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:32,000
One of the most powerful aspects of
VentureX is how CRM and Sales connect.

95
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:36,000
Go to Sarah Mitchell's contact page —
you'll see her company, her activities,

96
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:39,500
and the $45,000 Acme deal all in one view.

97
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:43,000
Click on Acme Corporation's company page
and you see Sarah, the deal,

98
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:46,000
and every logged activity.

99
00:05:46,100 --> 00:05:49,500
This interconnected data means your sales
team never loses context.

100
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:53,000
Every touchpoint, every deal,
every company — linked and searchable.

101
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:57,000
That's the CRM and Sales Pipeline
in VentureX.

102
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:00,500
You've learned how to manage companies,
contacts, activities, and your

103
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:04,000
entire sales funnel.
In the next video, we'll cover Inventory

104
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:07,500
and Procurement — how to manage your
products, stock levels, and purchase orders.

105
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:11,000
If you found this helpful,
like and subscribe for the next installment.
